Abstract

The Astrometric Proton Propulsion Simulation Engine (APP‑SE) is a multi‑scale simulation architecture designed to model laser‑driven proton propulsion within astrophysically realistic environments. By integrating plasma physics solvers, cosmic data ingestion, magnetic nozzle dynamics, and high‑performance computing, APP‑SE provides a unified framework for studying proton acceleration and thrust formation under interstellar and galactic field conditions. This work outlines the physical basis, computational architecture, and scientific intent of APP‑SE as a next‑generation propulsion research instrument.

1. Introduction

Laser‑driven proton acceleration has emerged as a promising pathway toward high‑specific‑impulse propulsion. Conventional simulations assume laboratory isolation, neglecting the influence of cosmic‑scale fields and fluxes. APP‑SE addresses this gap by embedding the propulsion model inside astrophysical environments, enabling investigation of how interstellar medium (ISM) density, cosmic‑ray spectra, and magnetic topology modulate proton beam formation and thrust stability.

2. Physical Model

2.1 Lorentz Force Framework

Proton motion is governed by the Lorentz force: [ F = q(\mathbf{E} + \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B}) ] where ( \mathbf{E} ) and ( \mathbf{B} ) arise from both laser‑driven plasma dynamics and cosmic‑scale fields.

2.2 Proton Acceleration Mechanisms

APP‑SE models two primary mechanisms:

TNSA — electron‑sheath acceleration

RPA — radiation‑pressure acceleration

2.3 Magnetic Nozzle Dynamics

Magnetic fields shape and collimate the proton beam, enabling thrust vector control, divergence reduction, and efficiency optimization.

3. Cosmic Data Ingestion

3.1 Dataset Sources

APP‑SE ingests astrophysical datasets including:

Cosmic‑ray spectra

ISM density maps

Solar wind telemetry

Galactic magnetic field surveys

GEANT4 particle‑interaction archives

3.2 Processing Pipeline

Data undergoes WCS coordinate mapping, unit normalization, spatial resampling, and noise filtering. The resulting fields provide boundary conditions for plasma density, magnetic topology, flux gradients, and turbulence.

4. Simulation Architecture

4.1 Core Solvers

APP‑SE integrates:

Particle‑in‑Cell (PIC) plasma evolution

Boris relativistic particle pusher

Yee lattice Maxwell solver

Coulomb and Bremsstrahlung collision operators

MHD coupling for large‑scale field behavior

4.2 Engine Dynamics Module

This module models proton injection, acceleration cavity behavior, magnetic nozzle geometry, thrust vector control, and beam shaping.

5. Computational Infrastructure

APP‑SE executes on heterogeneous HPC systems using MPI parallelism, CUDA/HIP GPU acceleration, and SLURM/PBS scheduling. Profiling tools monitor solver stability, memory bandwidth, and performance bottlenecks.

6. Security and Integrity

Scientific computation is secured via AES‑256‑GCM encryption, TLS 1.3 with hardware security modules, Merkle‑tree checkpoint verification, and post‑quantum key management.

7. Representative Simulation Scenario

A typical APP‑SE run models a 10–20 MeV proton source accelerated by a 35 fs, 800 nm, (3 \times 10^{19}) W/cm² laser pulse within a star‑forming molecular cloud environment. Outputs include proton energy spectra, thrust curves, beam divergence, nozzle efficiency, and stability under cosmic‑scale fields.

8. Discussion

APP‑SE demonstrates that cosmic‑embedded propulsion modeling reveals non‑trivial interactions between proton beams and astrophysical fields. Cosmic‑ray flux broadens energy distributions, ISM magnetic topology influences nozzle collimation, and turbulence modulates thrust stability. These results suggest that future propulsion concepts may benefit from environmental field coupling.

9. Conclusion

APP‑SE establishes a new class of propulsion simulation instrument that merges astrophysics, plasma physics, and engine dynamics. By situating proton propulsion inside realistic cosmic environments, APP‑SE enables exploration of field‑controlled, high‑specific‑impulse propulsion concepts with potential relevance to interstellar mission design.

